    \u0432\u043a\u043b\u044e\u0447\u0430\u044f \u043f\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0438\u044e \u0412\u043e\u043b\u044c\u0444\u0430
    \u043f\u043e \u043c\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043c\u0430\u0442\u0438\u043a\u0435 (1979), \u043f\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0438\u044e
    \u0421\u0442\u0438\u043b\u0430 (1980) \u0438 \u043f\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0438\u044e \u041a\u0438\u043e\u0442\u043e
    (1994). \u041e\u043d \u0431\u044b\u043b \u0447\u043b\u0435\u043d\u043e\u043c \u0424\u0440\u0430\u043d\u0446\u0443\u0437\u0441\u043a\u043e\u0439
    \u0430\u043a\u0430\u0434\u0435\u043c\u0438\u0438 \u043d\u0430\u0443\u043a \u0438 \u0438\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0440\u0430\u043d\u043d\u044b\u043c
    \u0447\u043b\u0435\u043d\u043e\u043c \u041b\u043e\u043d\u0434\u043e\u043d\u0441\u043a\u043e\u0433\u043e
    \u043a\u043e\u0440\u043e\u043b\u0435\u0432\u0441\u043a\u043e\u0433\u043e \u043e\u0431\u0449\u0435\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0430."
  achievementsEn: "The scientific legacy of Andre Veil is enormous and covers several fundamental areas of mathematics, and its main contribution is to create a solid foundation for algebraic geometry and to establish deep links between numbers theory and geometry, which has determined the development of mathematics for decades to come.\n\nThe founding of the Burbaki and the reform of mathematics was one of Veil's most important organizational achievements, and was involved in the formation of the group Nicole Burbaki in the 1930's, an ambitious project by a group of young French mathematicians who set out to re-write all mathematics on a single strict basis, building on the theory of multiples.The series \"Elements of Mathematics\", which Weil and his associates were working on, has had a tremendous impact on mathematics and science in the twentieth century, setting new standards of strictness and axiomatic approach.\n\n▪ Rethinking algebraic geometry before Weil's work, algebraic geometry, studying geometric objects assigned by algebraic equations, suffered from a lack of severity, and Weil took over titanic work on its restructuring at a modern theoretical-multiple base.His thesis, which was protected in 1928 under the leadership of Jacques Adamar and Emile Picard, was devoted to diofant geometry and is still in classical textbooks, and later continued to do so, allowing for the use of algebraic geometry in other fields of mathematics.\n\n~ Veil's hypotheses: The bridge between the discrete and the continuous Versailles of his scientific creation was the famous Veil hypotheses he had put forward in the late 1940s, and these hypotheses established a deep and unexpected connection between two seemingly distant worlds: the Discreted World of algebraic diversitys, set by equations over the final fields, a continuous world of topology, studying the properties of forms and spaces.In fact, Weil predicted that the number of solutions to equations over the final fields could be understood by studying the topological properties of their consistent spaces. The proof of these hypotheses, carried out in the 1970s by Alexander Grotendik and Pierre Delinim, was one of the most significant events in the mathematics of the 20th century and gave the Delina Fields Prize.\n\nOther fundamental areas besides algebraic geometry, Weil has made a key contribution to a number of other areas: Theory of Numbers: He has applied functional analysis and homologic algebra techniques to numbers theory, in particular by developing Galois's theory of commology. His work on Basic Number Theory has become a classic textbook. He has also obtained important results on Dirichle series and their definition through functional equations.Topological groups and integration: Weil developed a theory of integration into topological groups, which has been applied in harmonic analysis, and his monograph, Integration in Topological Groups and its Application, has been translated into Russian; the group theory and algebras: he studied Lee's groups and their discrete subgroups, as well as algebras with innovations and their relationship to classical groups.Vale contributed to their theory, the results were set out in the book Introduction to the Cable Diversity Theory.\n\nThe philosophy and interdisciplinary science of Veil was shaped by the structuralism that he took from Claude Levy-Stross. He wrote a mathematical annex to his famous work, \"Elementary structures of kinship\", which shows his interest in applying mathematical methods outside the traditional disciplines.\n\nThe recognition of the scale of his contribution was marked by many prestigious awards, including the Wolfe Prize in Mathematics (1979), the Steel Award (1980) and the Kyoto Prize (1994), and he was a member of the French Academy of Sciences and a foreign member of the Royal Society of London."
  personalLifeRu: "\u0412\u043e\u0442 \u043f\u043e\u0434\u0440\u043e\u0431\u043d\u044b\u0439 \u0440\u0430\u0441\u0441\u043a\u0430\u0437
    \u043e \u0436\u0438\u0437\u043d\u0435\u043d\u043d\u043e\u043c \u043f\u0443\u0442\u0438 \u0410\u043d\u0434\u0440\u0435
    \u0412\u0435\u0439\u043b\u044f: \u043e\u0442 \u0434\u0435\u0442\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0430
