    \u043d\u0430 \u0434\u0435\u0441\u044f\u0442\u0438\u043b\u0435\u0442\u0438\u044f \u0432\u043f\u0435\u0440\u0451\u0434."
  shortBioEn: "André Weil (1906–1998) was not only one of the greatest mathematicians of the 20th century, but also a man of extraordinary broad intellectual interests, whose life was closely intertwined with the history, philosophy and dramatic events of his time, his detailed biography, and his childhood and personality formation (1906–1920) was born on May 6, 1906 in Paris, in an educated and wealthy Jewish family from Elsas.His father was a doctor, his mother received a good musical education; the atmosphere in the house was creative and intellectual; family friendships, such as with Nobel Laureate Ilya Mechnikov, were part of their cultural circle; since the early years, Andre had a unique ability to speak languages; parents spoke German freely; and children listened to them and learned language in early childhood.He studied ancient Greek, Latin, and, most importantly, Sanskrit, which he began to do on his own, and then under the leadership of the famous Professor Sylvain Levy, and later used Sanskrit to read ancient Indian philosophical texts that, although far from religion, influenced his world view and life decisions.It is worth noting that his younger sister, Simone Weil, later became a famous philosopher and mystic, and the first world war adjusted his education: his father was called by a military doctor and his family often moved, so Andre sometimes studied at home; however, his math skills were early; he himself mastered the school textbook of algebra and participated in challenge competitions for the Journal of Elementary Mathematics.After completing his high school early in 1933, Weil entered France's most prestigious university, the Higher Normal School (Eócole Normal Supérieure), where he not only studied mathematics in depth, including the work of Riman, but also continued to study Sanskrit.In 1925, he completed his studies and in 1928, under the leadership of Jacques Adamard and Emile Picard, he defended his dissertation in diofant geometry, which he later entered into classical textbooks, and after protection, he received a scholarship to study science centres in Italy and Germany, and an important step was his introduction to Emmy Neuter, a great algebraist, who studied in Germany, which had a significant impact on his approach to mathematics.The Indian Odyssey and Burbaki (1930–1939) in France did not have suitable vacancies, and his Sanskrit mentor, Sylvain Levy, agreed to appoint Weil as head of the Department of Mathematics at Aligarh University in British India, a bold and unusual step for a young French mathematician.His stay in India (early 1930's) was an important personal experience: he travelled extensively, communicated with local intellectuals, including Mahatma Gandhi, but his attempts to reform teaching and recruit new staff failed because of local particularities and administrative intrigues, and he was fired after a year and a half, returning to France and receiving a seat at the University of Strasbourg (1933–1940).This is where he started his most important organizational activity, and together with a group of young like-minded people (Anri Cartan, Jean Djedonne, Claude Chevalle, and others), he was one of the founders of the legendary group Nicole Burbaki, whose aim was to re-articulate the basics of all mathematics from a common perspective, building on the theory of multiples, and to create a new and rigorous foundation for mathematical knowledge.The work on the multi-volume \"Mathematics Elements\" was a life-long work for Vale and his associates, and the war, emigration and mature years (1940–1998) with the outbreak of World War II, as a Jew, Weil was in danger, and he emigrated to the United States, where he began his work at the University of Pennsylvania (1941–1945), then at the University of São Paulo in Brazil (1945–1947), and then moved to the University of Chicago (1947–1958).His final career was at the Princeton Institute of Advanced Studies (1958–1976), where he remained until retirement and died on 6 August 1998, and he became a recognized leader of world mathematics during those years, and his main works are algebraic geometry, which he re-thinked and gave her strictness, and numbers and topology.He developed the theory of integration into topological groups and applied homologic algebra techniques to the theory of numbers (Galois's genomics), and his legacy: Veil's hypotheses and the scientific interests of Vershina's scientific creation became Veil's hypotheses (proved in the 1970s by Grotendik and Delinim).They have established a deep connection between the discrete world of algebraic diversity and the continuous world of topology, which was one of the most significant events in mathematics in the 20th century.He also spoke freely in many languages, including Russian, and lectured in Portuguese during his work in Brazil. André Weil was awarded many awards, including the Wolf Prize for Mathematics (1979), the Steel Award (1980) and the Kyoto Prize (1994).He was a member of the French Academy of Sciences and a foreign member of the Royal Society of London, and his life and work left an indelible mark on mathematics, determining its development for decades to come."
  interestingFactsRu: "\u0410\u043d\u0434\u0440\u0435 \u0412\u0435\u0439\u043b\u044c \u0431\u044b\u043b \u0432\u044b\u0434\u0430\u044e\u0449\u0438\u043c\u0441\u044f
